WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:04.060
If you want to hear the hidden meaning behind
these two CryptTV shorts, stick around to

00:00:04.060 --> 00:00:06.260
the end of this video.

00:00:06.260 --> 00:00:12.809
One of the best places to find high quality
short films right now is CryptTV.

00:00:12.809 --> 00:00:16.500
So today, I'm going to be showing you some
of my favorite Crypt short films and a few

00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:18.340
things you might have missed in each of them.

00:00:18.340 --> 00:00:19.910
This video is sponsored by Crypt TV.

00:00:19.910 --> 00:00:21.980
The first one I'm going to talk about is The
Mime.

00:00:21.980 --> 00:00:25.780
If you haven't seen it, click or tap right
over here to watch it, and if you need a refresher,

00:00:25.780 --> 00:00:29.080
it's about a woman's encounter with a creature
who mirrors her every move, and at the end

00:00:29.080 --> 00:00:33.200
of the short, she finds that she's the one
being controlled by the movements of the mime.

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:35.620
But how did The Mime get this power over her?

00:00:35.620 --> 00:00:39.050
My theory is that this tunnel has some kind
of power over those who pass through it.

00:00:39.050 --> 00:00:42.440
She's able to go right to the edge of the
tunnel before it takes over her movements.

00:00:42.440 --> 00:00:44.552
This little lip-balm tin seems to have something
to do with it.

00:00:44.552 --> 00:00:49.010
It was the last item to have fallen out of
her bag and she ends up leaving it behind.

00:00:49.010 --> 00:00:52.559
It's possible that she isn't able to leave
the tunnel without everything she came in

00:00:52.559 --> 00:00:54.170
with -- and here's why I think that.

00:00:54.170 --> 00:00:57.210
She spills her bag and picks up everything
but the lip-balm.

00:00:57.210 --> 00:00:59.250
She tries to leave before finishing the job.

00:00:59.250 --> 00:01:02.079
In this case, the job is picking up all of
her belongings.

00:01:02.079 --> 00:01:05.740
Now rewind to the beginning of the short,
before Before Abigail enters the tunnel.

00:01:05.740 --> 00:01:07.480
She's on the phone with a co-worker.

00:01:07.480 --> 00:01:10.100
It's tough to hear her over the traffic, so
I'll turn on the subtitles.

00:01:10.100 --> 00:01:13.660
"I was supposed to have it by five.

00:01:13.660 --> 00:01:17.670
So now I'm gonna have to wait until Monday.

00:01:17.670 --> 00:01:21.229
Okay, and it will be on my desk.

00:01:21.229 --> 00:01:22.229
At nine."

00:01:22.229 --> 00:01:26.040
One of her workers couldn't get the job done,
and thus, wasn't allowed to leave -- had to

00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:27.040
work overtime.

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:29.710
I think that this idea leads into the meaning
of the short as well.

00:01:29.710 --> 00:01:33.560
At the beginning Abigail is in a position
of power, controlling the movements of The

00:01:33.560 --> 00:01:37.180
Mime, but when The Mime outsmarts her, he's
promoted to the higher position of power,

00:01:37.180 --> 00:01:40.460
and now she has to follow his orders and she
pays the price for it.

00:01:40.460 --> 00:01:44.270
So the whole thing is an allegory for the
unrelenting abuse of the corporate ladder.

00:01:44.270 --> 00:01:48.100
The next short is a SciFi-Horror called Tethers,
in which a woman searching for her friends

00:01:48.100 --> 00:01:51.920
in the midst of an alien invasion, receives
a warning from a dying stranger.

00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:56.750
At the beginning she calls out the names of
her two friends.

00:01:56.750 --> 00:01:57.750
"David?

00:01:57.750 --> 00:01:58.750
Lynn?"

00:01:58.750 --> 00:02:02.500
Hearing those names in succession makes me
think of psychological horror director David

00:02:02.500 --> 00:02:06.370
Lynch, and it could also be an allusion to
the mind bending twist that's about to happen.

00:02:06.370 --> 00:02:09.940
The guy she encounters gives a series of warnings
that all turn out to be to be true.

00:02:09.940 --> 00:02:11.020
They're in the trees.

00:02:11.020 --> 00:02:12.880
They send out things that get in your head.

00:02:12.880 --> 00:02:14.580
Things that make you not yourself.

00:02:14.580 --> 00:02:15.700
Don't trust anyone.

00:02:15.700 --> 00:02:18.300
That last one plays with the idea of perspective.

00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:22.300
Usually movies are told from the protagonist's'
perspective, so when Sarah runs into David

00:02:22.300 --> 00:02:26.020
and Lynn, we expect the twist to be that they
are the ones taken over by the aliens, not

00:02:26.020 --> 00:02:28.470
the character we've been following this whole
time.

00:02:28.470 --> 00:02:31.750
And that's when we find out why the film is
called Tether, because they aliens have attached

00:02:31.750 --> 00:02:33.410
themselves to the main character.

00:02:33.410 --> 00:02:36.690
Both David and the stranger that we see at
the beginning drop their wallets when being

00:02:36.690 --> 00:02:40.660
possessed by the creatures, which is symbolic
of losing your identity when an outside force

00:02:40.660 --> 00:02:41.660
takes control.

00:02:41.660 --> 00:02:45.260
The next short is called Terra, and it follows
a woman who moves to the desert, where she's

00:02:45.260 --> 00:02:48.930
terrorized by her creepy nephew and a higher
being known as Terra.

00:02:48.930 --> 00:02:52.960
Terra is an Italian word meaning land or earth,
and the being in this film takes on a violent

00:02:52.960 --> 00:02:56.400
mother nature kind of role and uses her power
to protect her domain.

00:02:56.400 --> 00:03:00.130
The opening shot shows this white flower being
turning red, almost as if it's being painted

00:03:00.130 --> 00:03:04.014
with blood, and drawing a comparison of the
pollution of the earth to the gore of a human

00:03:04.014 --> 00:03:07.620
being killed, which is a comparison we continue
to see throughout the movie as Terra gives

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:10.490
the enemies of the environment a taste of
their own medicine.

00:03:10.490 --> 00:03:14.420
I also interpreted this water droplet to be
a teardrop shed by Mother Nature.

00:03:14.420 --> 00:03:16.790
So we have two opposite characters in this
film.

00:03:16.790 --> 00:03:20.320
Rachel, who is wasting water by running her
sprinkler in the day, running the washing

00:03:20.320 --> 00:03:24.480
machine with only one item, leaving the faucet
running and that's not even half of the water

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:25.980
she's wasting during this movie.

00:03:25.980 --> 00:03:29.480
All of this is amplified by the fact that
she just moved to the desert where water is

00:03:29.480 --> 00:03:30.760
a lot harder to come by.

00:03:30.760 --> 00:03:34.360
We know she just moved because she tells her
sister on the phone that land is stupid cheap

00:03:34.360 --> 00:03:36.520
here, and she still has boxes to unpack.

00:03:36.520 --> 00:03:40.820
She represents the European settlers in America
who moved out, took over the land and continue

00:03:40.820 --> 00:03:42.340
to destroy the environment.

00:03:42.340 --> 00:03:44.730
Then there's her nephew, who is not at all
wasteful.

00:03:44.730 --> 00:03:48.240
When we first see him he's hitting spoons
against a vase like a native tribesman.

00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:52.150
He represents the Native Americans who respect
the environment and who were negatively affected

00:03:52.150 --> 00:03:54.010
by the industrialization of America.

00:03:54.010 --> 00:03:57.960
He can be seen shutting off the running water
and the only time he uses water is to drink

00:03:57.960 --> 00:03:58.960
it.

00:03:58.960 --> 00:04:01.840
When Rachel is discussing with her sister
about letting Noah possibly stay another day,

00:04:01.840 --> 00:04:03.660
she mentions her great maternal instincts.

00:04:03.660 --> 00:04:06.120
However, we find that not to really be true.

00:04:06.120 --> 00:04:10.330
She has no idea how to interact with the kid
and he creeps her out.

00:04:10.330 --> 00:04:11.460
"Weird kid."

00:04:11.460 --> 00:04:16.780
But I did find it interesting that she chose
the term "maternal instincts", almost a reference

00:04:16.780 --> 00:04:20.070
to Mother Nature, which she also does not
fare well with.

00:04:20.070 --> 00:04:23.570
Earlier I talked about the white flower being
stained with a red, blood-like substance to

00:04:23.570 --> 00:04:25.780
represent Rachel "murdering" the environment.

00:04:25.780 --> 00:04:29.630
We see this happen to the flowers around the
house as well as she continues to waste more

00:04:29.630 --> 00:04:30.630
water.

00:04:30.630 --> 00:04:32.850
One of the parallels I mentioned is on Rachel
herself.

00:04:32.850 --> 00:04:36.510
Everything she wears before her death is white,
but in the scene where she pushes Terra over

00:04:36.510 --> 00:04:40.450
the edge to take her life, she removes the
white bathrobe to reveal a crimson colored

00:04:40.450 --> 00:04:41.480
bathing suit.

00:04:41.480 --> 00:04:45.150
After her death the water leaving her body
turns to blood, which soaks back into the

00:04:45.150 --> 00:04:46.150
flowers.

00:04:46.150 --> 00:04:49.910
Noah recites the line, "from ashes to ashes,"
a biblical text referencing how our bodies

00:04:49.910 --> 00:04:51.460
return to the earth after death.

00:04:51.460 --> 00:04:55.840
I also find it interesting how Terra attacks
Rachel by using her own blows to the environment

00:04:55.840 --> 00:04:56.840
against her.

00:04:56.840 --> 00:05:00.790
For example, she first boils Rachels wine,
just as Rachel boiled the water for the coffee

00:05:00.790 --> 00:05:02.130
the never even made.

00:05:02.130 --> 00:05:05.900
Then in the hot tub, Terra takes back all
the wasted water from Rachel's body, leaving

00:05:05.900 --> 00:05:09.760
her gasping for a drink even though she's
surrounded by the water of the jacuzzi.

00:05:09.760 --> 00:05:13.060
In direct contrast to this, is the nephew,
Noah, who is drinking water out of his own

00:05:13.060 --> 00:05:17.880
cup, which by the way is green, just like
his wardrobe, and everyone knows green means

00:05:17.880 --> 00:05:20.950
environmentally friendly, right?

00:05:20.950 --> 00:05:21.990
RIGHT?!

00:05:21.990 --> 00:05:26.160
At the end, Terra tells Noah that she will
only hurt him if he hurts her first, more

00:05:26.160 --> 00:05:28.740
evidence for her being a Mother Nature type
of being.

00:05:28.740 --> 00:05:32.490
And just as Terra took back the water out
of Rachel's body, the next victim is also

00:05:32.490 --> 00:05:36.370
punished with the same tactic that he used
to hurt the environment, when Terra changes

00:05:36.370 --> 00:05:40.830
forms into a fire monster to attack the guy
who started a wildfire in the desert by dropping

00:05:40.830 --> 00:05:41.830
his cigarette.

00:05:41.830 --> 00:05:45.920
CryptTV also uploads some animated horror
films from time to time, like this one, Happy

00:05:45.920 --> 00:05:46.920
Meal Horror.

00:05:46.920 --> 00:05:50.340
It's made my a studio called Dark Vessel who
often makes films where the characters are

00:05:50.340 --> 00:05:53.830
all living beings that look like robots, so
if you're wondering why all the characters

00:05:53.830 --> 00:05:55.740
are robots, that's just their style.

00:05:55.740 --> 00:05:58.360
This video is packed with references to other
horror movies.

00:05:58.360 --> 00:06:01.880
The name of the place is Hooper's Meat, which
is an homage to the late Tobe Hooper, the

00:06:01.880 --> 00:06:05.990
director of The Texas Chainsaw Massacre, and
Happy Meal Horror ends up having a little

00:06:05.990 --> 00:06:07.880
chainsaw massacre of its own.

00:06:07.880 --> 00:06:11.920
There are also a lot of references to Stanley
Kubrick films as well, such as the main antagonist,

00:06:11.920 --> 00:06:15.950
this robot, having the eye of HAL from 2001:
A Space Odyssey and the floor of the meat

00:06:15.950 --> 00:06:20.290
plant having the same design as the carpet
in the Overlook Hotel from The Shining.

00:06:20.290 --> 00:06:24.580
This shot also doubles as a reference to Insidious,
which often uses the imagery of a red door

00:06:24.580 --> 00:06:27.400
at the end of a blue tinted hallway in the
Further.

00:06:27.400 --> 00:06:31.210
That's all before we even get to the titles,
which use the same font as American Horror

00:06:31.210 --> 00:06:32.210
Story.

00:06:32.210 --> 00:06:35.420
One little tidbit I found amusing is as the
cow is running away, there is a row of hanging

00:06:35.420 --> 00:06:40.210
human skeletons, which brings up plenty of
questions, like are they using human meat

00:06:40.210 --> 00:06:41.730
in their meals as well?

00:06:41.730 --> 00:06:43.090
Are there humans in this world?

00:06:43.090 --> 00:06:46.370
Or is that what the humanoid robots looks
like on the inside?

00:06:46.370 --> 00:06:50.580
There's also a section with some chickens
as well, and they look more like rubber chickens.

00:06:50.580 --> 00:06:53.980
And just a couple of fun notes, the robot
has a name-tag which reads: "EaRL", but it

00:06:53.980 --> 00:06:56.700
doesn't appear to be a "My name is" style
name-tag.

00:06:56.700 --> 00:07:00.560
This is also one of the few Crypt shorts to
have an animated title sequence.

00:07:00.560 --> 00:07:03.050
The last one I'm gonna look at today is called
Selfish.

00:07:03.050 --> 00:07:06.120
In order to understand this short, keep in
mind that everything that happens relates

00:07:06.120 --> 00:07:07.610
back to this opening scene.

00:07:07.610 --> 00:07:10.480
"You know the other night when we were at
Steve's?

00:07:10.480 --> 00:07:12.650
We were talking about online dating.

00:07:12.650 --> 00:07:15.370
You know, just chilling, discussing Tinder.

00:07:15.370 --> 00:07:17.639
And what does she say?

00:07:17.639 --> 00:07:22.530
She said something like, "Beauty's all about
being comfortable in your own skin.

00:07:22.530 --> 00:07:25.760
Oh my God, can you believe that?"

00:07:25.760 --> 00:07:29.430
The film presents us with two characters with
a twisted sense of beauty, characters who

00:07:29.430 --> 00:07:32.760
can't see value in anything without makeup
and filters.

00:07:32.760 --> 00:07:36.139
The horror of this short film comes when the
artificial beauty that they are so obsessed

00:07:36.139 --> 00:07:40.780
with goes too far far and turns them into
monsters, in this case: literally.

00:07:40.780 --> 00:07:44.450
As they are mocking this other girl and applying
their filters, Phoebe's eyes tweak out into

00:07:44.450 --> 00:07:46.360
something terrifying momentarily.

00:07:46.360 --> 00:07:49.740
It's easy to miss, but it's a sign of everything
to come when the filters start to consume

00:07:49.740 --> 00:07:50.740
them.

00:07:50.740 --> 00:07:53.870
There's also this picture on the wall, which,
here I'm gonna turn up the contrast.

00:07:53.870 --> 00:07:57.669
It's pretty creepy and it's a perfect example
of the girls' screwed perception of beauty.

00:07:57.669 --> 00:08:01.230
Katie claims that one of the filters looks
like Lady Gaga, and starts singing the lyrics

00:08:01.230 --> 00:08:02.230
to Bad Romance.

00:08:02.230 --> 00:08:06.490
"I want your ugly, I want your disease."

00:08:06.490 --> 00:08:09.300
Well... she botches the lyrics a little bit.

00:08:09.300 --> 00:08:10.830
"Ooh la la.

00:08:10.830 --> 00:08:12.360
Want your performance.

00:08:12.360 --> 00:08:13.360
Mmm...

00:08:13.360 --> 00:08:14.360
Hm...

00:08:14.360 --> 00:08:15.360
Mmm, mmm.

00:08:15.360 --> 00:08:16.360
hmmm."

00:08:16.360 --> 00:08:18.520
Probably to avoid getting a copystrike from
Interscope Records... but I think Bad Romance

00:08:18.520 --> 00:08:22.830
is an ironic song for this character, because
I'd interpret Bad Romance to be a song about

00:08:22.830 --> 00:08:26.990
an ugly romance, as opposed to the typical
fairytale romance you might find in Hollywood

00:08:26.990 --> 00:08:29.100
blockbusters and Top 40 music.

00:08:29.100 --> 00:08:32.840
With lyrics like 'I want your horror; I want
your design .'Cause you're a criminal as long

00:08:32.840 --> 00:08:36.650
as you're mine, Gaga is telling her lover
that he's not perfect, but she doesn't want

00:08:36.650 --> 00:08:37.840
him any other way.

00:08:37.840 --> 00:08:41.770
And there are other horror references in the
song like 'I want your Psycho, your Vertigo

00:08:41.770 --> 00:08:42.770
shtick.

00:08:42.770 --> 00:08:43.770
Want you in my Rear Window.

00:08:43.770 --> 00:08:44.770
Baby you're sick.'

00:08:44.770 --> 00:08:47.950
They're all references to famous horror films
directed by Alfred Hitchcock.

00:08:47.950 --> 00:08:51.190
But I'm getting sidetracked here, this is
'Things You Missed In CryptTV Shorts', not

00:08:51.190 --> 00:08:52.880
'Things You Missed In Lady Gaga Songs'.

00:08:52.880 --> 00:08:56.260
The main point to take away, is that the girls
are being hypocritical by singing a song like

00:08:56.260 --> 00:09:00.290
Bad Romance, yet relying on crutches like
the filters to try to change their appearance.

00:09:00.290 --> 00:09:04.420
I mean look at this one, it completely covers
her face, I don't know why that's desirable.

00:09:04.420 --> 00:09:08.300
And going too far with the filters, Katie
eventually turns into one of those monsters,

00:09:08.300 --> 00:09:12.360
and that's where the music makes a change
to a song from the 1920s by an artist named

00:09:12.360 --> 00:09:13.370
Asa Martin.

00:09:13.370 --> 00:09:17.350
The name of the song is "She Ain't Built That
Way", and the lyrics talk about how the stereotypical

00:09:17.350 --> 00:09:21.771
tendencies of a 1920s woman, one of the verses
details how some women are lying with their

00:09:21.771 --> 00:09:22.771
appearance.

00:09:22.771 --> 00:09:23.771
"With bustles and padding and heels that are
high,

00:09:23.771 --> 00:09:24.771
And blending of color just to capture the
eye,

00:09:24.771 --> 00:09:29.850
But the real from the sham you tell if you
try

00:09:29.850 --> 00:09:35.620
Because there's lots of them built that way."

00:09:35.620 --> 00:09:39.620
So the song is talking about the artificial
way women try to enhance their beauty before

00:09:39.620 --> 00:09:43.890
Instagram filters were a thing: bustles to
give the appearance of wider hips, padding

00:09:43.890 --> 00:09:46.000
the bra, wearing high heels and blending makeup.

00:09:46.000 --> 00:09:49.440
And the singer claims that you can tell the
real from the fake, but lots of women try

00:09:49.440 --> 00:09:50.740
to use this deception.

00:09:50.740 --> 00:09:54.490
The message of this verse is exactly the message
of the short film, it was just made about

00:09:54.490 --> 00:09:55.520
100 years earlier.

00:09:55.520 --> 00:09:59.250
On Phoebe's dresser you can see all the beauty
products, and there are also a couple dolls

00:09:59.250 --> 00:10:00.320
in the room as well.

00:10:00.320 --> 00:10:03.620
Dolls are another item, like filters, that
are supposed to be cute, but sometimes are

00:10:03.620 --> 00:10:05.360
overdone and end up looking pretty creepy.

00:10:05.360 --> 00:10:08.320
There's a whole genre of creepy doll movies
predicated on this.

00:10:08.320 --> 00:10:11.990
You'll also notice a couple mirrors, which
have the same use as the front facing smartphone

00:10:11.990 --> 00:10:14.550
camera, it's all about self validation.

00:10:14.550 --> 00:10:18.620
Katie eventually finds Pheobe in her room
and she's now become the full monster version

00:10:18.620 --> 00:10:19.910
of the little glimpse we saw earlier.

00:10:19.910 --> 00:10:24.010
They are both consumed by filtering themselves
into something they are not, and they both

00:10:24.010 --> 00:10:25.390
end up looking horrifying.

00:10:25.390 --> 00:10:26.980
Eh, eh.

00:10:26.980 --> 00:10:28.610
There's nothing else I can say.

00:10:28.610 --> 00:10:31.420
Because that's the end of this episode of
Things You Missed.

00:10:31.420 --> 00:10:34.899
The way you blowin' up my phone won't make
me heart your comment, but feel free to go

